= 1
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Write the remaining <quadratic polynomial> as $x^2+bx+c$. Comparing coefficients in $(x-2)(x^2+bx+c)$ with the given <cubic polynomial> gives $b-2=-1$ and $-2c=-2$, hence $b=c=1$. The coefficient of $x$ is then $c-2b=-1$, as required. Thus
$$
\boxed{x^3-x^2-x-2=(x-2)(x^2+x+1),\qquad\text{other factor }x^2+x+1.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <Pythagorean trigonometric identity> makes the second factor $1+\cos^2\theta$, which is strictly positive. It is therefore enough to solve $\sin\theta-\cos\theta\le0$. The <sine addition formula> gives
$$
\sin\theta-\cos\theta=\sqrt2\sin(\theta-\pi/4).
$$
The shifted <angle> lies between $\pi/4$ and $5\pi/4$. On this interval the <sine> is nonpositive precisely from $\pi$ onwards. Restoring the original <angle> and retaining its strict upper bound gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{5\pi}{4}\le\theta<\frac{3\pi}{2}.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Apply the <product rule> to $x\sin(1/x)$ and the <chain rule> to the <sine>. Since $d(1/x)/dx=-1/x^2$, the <derivative> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=\sin\frac1x-\frac1x\cos\frac1x,\qquad x\ne0.}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Set $X=x^2$ and $Y=y^2$ in the two <conic sections>. A real intersection requires $X,Y\ge0$. Solving the resulting <linear equations> gives
$$
X=\frac3{2a+1},\qquad Y=\frac{a-1}{2a+1}.
$$
For $a>0$ the denominator and $X$ are positive, so intersections exist exactly when $a\ge1$. At $a=1$ they are $(\pm1,0)$; for $a>1$ both choices of sign for each square root give intersections. Consequently
$$
\boxed{0<a<1\quad\text{gives no shared points}.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <double-angle formula> for the <tangent function> gives $1=2t/(1-t^2)$. Here $0<t<1$, because $0<\pi/8<\pi/4$, so the denominator is nonzero. Rearranging yields the <quadratic equation>
$$
\boxed{t^2+2t-1=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <quadratic formula> gives $t=-1\pm\sqrt2$. The <tangent function> is positive in the first quadrant, so the positive <polynomial root> is selected:
$$
\boxed{\tan\frac\pi8=\sqrt2-1.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Use <integration by substitution> with $u=3-2x$, so $dx=-du/2$ and $x=(3-u)/2$. The <indefinite integral> becomes
$$
-\frac14\int(3u^{1/2}-u^{3/2})\,du=-\frac12u^{3/2}+\frac1{10}u^{5/2}+C.
$$
Thus, with an arbitrary <constant of integration>,
$$
\boxed{\int x\sqrt{3-2x}\,dx=-\frac{x+1}{5}(3-2x)^{3/2}+C.}
$$
This real <antiderivative> applies for $x<3/2$ and extends continuously to the stated endpoint. Its left <derivative> there agrees with the limiting integrand, zero.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

The two directed segments have <vectors> $\mathbf u=(1,0,-1)$ and $\mathbf v=(0,1,1)$. Their <dot product> is $-1$ and both <norms> equal $\sqrt2$. Hence
$$
\cos\theta=\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{|\mathbf u||\mathbf v|}=-\frac12,
\qquad\boxed{\theta=\frac{2\pi}3=120^\circ.}
$$
The common starting point fixes the obtuse <angle> between these segments.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Differentiate the two powers to locate the <stationary point>:
$$
y'=\frac{x-2}{x^3}.
$$
For $x>0$, this is negative before $2$ and positive afterwards, so the <global minimum> is
$$
\boxed{x=2,\qquad y=-\frac14.}
$$
The curve crosses the horizontal axis at $x=1$. Its <limits> are $+\infty$ as $x\to0^+$ and $0$ from below as $x\to\infty$, giving the vertical <asymptote> $x=0$ and horizontal <asymptote> $y=0$. These features determine the required sketch.

\Image[paper-1-2020-q8-reciprocal-curve.png]
{title=Positive branch of the reciprocal curve, with its minimum and axis crossing}

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The first term is $ar$, so the <finite geometric series> is $S=a(r+r^2+\cdots+r^{100})$. Subtracting $rS$ cancels the interior terms and gives $(1-r)S=ar(1-r^{100})$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{S=\frac{ar(1-r^{100})}{1-r}\quad(r\ne1);\qquad S=100a\quad(r=1).}
$$
The rational expression holds for every real $a$ and every real $r\ne1$, including $r=0$; the same algebra works for <complex numbers>. No restriction $|r|<1$ is needed for a <finite geometric series>. The exceptional case follows by adding one hundred equal terms, or by taking the <limit> as $r\to1$.

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

For this <polynomial>, $f'(x)=2x$, so the specified <Newton root-finding iteration> simplifies to
$$
x_{n+1}=\frac12\left(x_n+\frac2{x_n}\right).
$$
Starting at one gives $x_1=(1+2)/2=3/2$, followed by $x_2=(3/2+4/3)/2=17/12$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{x_1=1.5,\qquad x_2\approx1.42\text{ to two decimal places}.}
$$

