= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate> $2+i$:
$$
\frac1{2-i}=\frac{2+i}{(2-i)(2+i)}=\frac{2+i}5.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are
$$
\boxed{\Re z=\frac25,\qquad\Im z=\frac15.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Powers of the <imaginary unit> repeat in blocks $i,-1,-i,1$, each with sum zero. The first $44$ terms comprise eleven complete blocks; the last is $i^{45}=i$. Applying <Euler's formula> to the resulting <complex exponential> gives $e^i=\cos1+i\sin1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\Re z=\cos1,\qquad\Im z=\sin1.}
$$
The <angles> here are in <radians>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The exponential definitions of <hyperbolic sine> and <hyperbolic cosine>, together with <Euler's formula>, give $\sinh(iu)=i\sin u$ and $\cosh(iu)=\cos u$. The argument of the <complex logarithm> therefore reduces to $i\sin(\pi/2)+\cos(9\pi/2)=i$.

Using the <principal logarithm>, $\log i=i\pi/2$, so
$$
\boxed{\Re z=0,\qquad\Im z=\frac\pi2\quad\text{(principal value)}.}
$$
If no branch of the <complex logarithm> is selected, its complete set of values is $i(\pi/2+2\pi k)$, $k\in\mathbb Z$. The <real part> remains zero, while the <imaginary part> is $\pi/2+2\pi k$. The original paper does not explicitly state a branch convention.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The literal expression in the original PDF is undefined. Indeed, <hyperbolic tangent> satisfies $\tanh w=\sinh w/\cosh w$, and
$$
\cosh(in\pi/4)=\cos(n\pi/4)=0\quad\text{when }n\equiv2\pmod4.
$$
Already the $n=2$ summand contains $\tanh(i\pi/2)$, a <pole>. The shifted term has the same issue when $n\equiv3\pmod4$. Thus this is not a finite sum of defined <complex numbers>, and it has **no literal real or imaginary part**. Cancelling two undefined terms is not ordinary arithmetic.

There is nevertheless a natural interpretation by <meromorphic telescoping cancellation>. Insert the same small positive real shift $\varepsilon$ into every <hyperbolic tangent> argument. Each term is then finite, and the <telescoping sum> is
$$
S(\varepsilon)=\tanh(\varepsilon+121i\pi/4)-\tanh\varepsilon.
$$
Since <hyperbolic tangent> has period $i\pi$, its first argument can be reduced to $\varepsilon+i\pi/4$. Taking the <limit> gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{\varepsilon\to0^+}S(\varepsilon)=i,\qquad\Re=0,\quad\Im=1.}
$$
This is a common-shift continuation of the sum, plausibly the intended telescoping answer. It requires that additional convention; it is not the value of the expression as printed.

