= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $z=x+iy$. Then $|z^*+2i|=\sqrt{x^2+(y-2)^2}$ and $|z|=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, so the condition fixes the sum of distances from $(0,2)$ and $(0,0)$. It is an <ellipse> with centre $(0,1)$, vertical semimajor axis $A=2$, and focal distance $c=1$. Its horizontal semiminor axis obeys $B^2=A^2-c^2=3$. Thus the <locus> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{x^2}{3}+\frac{(y-1)^2}{4}=1.}
$$
The vertices are $(0,-1)$ and $(0,3)$; the horizontal extremities are $(\pm\sqrt3,1)$. The <complex conjugation> places the second focus above the origin.

\Image[paper-1-2020-q11-complex-loci.png]
{title=Distance-sum loci in the complex plane: an ellipse and its limiting segment}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <triangle inequality> bounds the distance sum below by the separation of the two foci, which is two. Equality holds exactly for points on the closed segment joining them. Hence the <locus> is
$$
\boxed{x=0,\qquad0\le y\le2,\quad\text{equivalently }z=iy\ (0\le y\le2).}
$$
Both endpoints are included. It is the segment on the positive <imaginary axis> shown in the right-hand panel above.

