= Solution
{parent=a}

For <polar coordinates>, $x=r\cos\theta$ and $y=r\sin\theta$. The <Jacobian determinant> is $r$, so the <area element> is
$$
\boxed{dS=r\,dr\,d\theta\qquad(r\ge0).}
$$
This accounts for the increasing length of an angular strip as its distance from the origin increases.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $I$ be the positive <Gaussian integral>. Its <improper integral> converges because $e^{-x^2}\le e^{-|x|}$ for $|x|\ge1$. Multiplying two copies and using the <polar coordinates> <area element> gives
$$
I^2=\int_{\mathbb R^2}e^{-(x^2+y^2)}\,dx\,dy
=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\infty e^{-r^2}r\,dr\,d\theta
=2\pi\left[-\frac12e^{-r^2}\right]_0^\infty=\pi.
$$
Nonnegativity justifies the product <double integral> and the change of integration order. Since $I>0$,
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2}\,dx=\sqrt\pi.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <cardioid> has a nonnegative radial boundary, so each ray from the origin contributes $0\le r\le a(1+\cos\theta)$. Integrating the <polar coordinates> <area element> yields
$$
A=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{a(1+\cos\theta)}r\,dr\,d\theta
=\frac{a^2}2\int_0^{2\pi}(1+2\cos\theta+\cos^2\theta)\,d\theta.
$$
The three angular integrals are $2\pi$, zero and $\pi$, respectively, using the <double-angle formula> for the last. Thus
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{3\pi a^2}{2}.}
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <volume element> is the absolute <Jacobian determinant> times the coordinate differentials. In the <Cartesian coordinate system>, <cylindrical coordinates> $(r,\theta,z)$ and <spherical coordinates> $(\rho,\vartheta,\varphi)$, respectively,
$$
\boxed{dV=dx\,dy\,dz,\qquad dV=r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz,\qquad dV=\rho^2\sin\vartheta\,d\rho\,d\vartheta\,d\varphi.}
$$
Here $\rho$ is distance from the origin, $\vartheta$ is the polar <angle> from the positive $z$ axis, and $\varphi$ is the azimuthal <angle>. The <spherical coordinates> convention fixes which <angle> enters the <sine> factor.

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

At height $z>0$, the <elliptic paraboloid> has a circular cross-section $0\le r\le\sqrt z$, of <area> $\pi z$. The <cylindrical coordinates> <volume element> therefore reduces the <volume integral> to
$$
K=\int_0^\infty\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\sqrt z}f'(z)r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz
=\pi\int_0^\infty zf'(z)\,dz.
$$
For the specified $f$, the <derivative> is $e^{-z}(z^{-1/2}+\tfrac12z^{-3/2})$. Thus $zf'(z)$ is integrable at zero and decays exponentially at infinity. Apply <integration by parts> first on a truncated interval and then take the endpoint <limits>:
$$
K=\pi[zf(z)]_0^\infty-\pi\int_0^\infty f(z)\,dz
=\pi\int_0^\infty\frac{e^{-z}}{\sqrt z}\,dz.
$$
Both boundary terms vanish because $zf(z)=-\sqrt z\,e^{-z}$. With $z=u^2$, <integration by substitution> gives the <Gaussian integral>:
$$
\boxed{K=2\pi\int_0^\infty e^{-u^2}\,du=\pi^{3/2}.}
$$
The integrand is singular at the apex but the resulting <improper integral> is convergent.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In <spherical coordinates>, the <cone> condition forces $z\ge0$ and becomes $0\le\vartheta\le\alpha$. The unit <ball> gives $0\le\rho\le1$. Hence
$$
V=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\alpha\int_0^1\rho^2\sin\vartheta\,d\rho\,d\vartheta\,d\varphi
=\boxed{\frac{2\pi}{3}(1-\cos\alpha).}
$$
For an independent calculation in <cylindrical coordinates>, the lower boundary is $z=r\cot\alpha$ and the upper boundary is $z=\sqrt{1-r^2}$. They meet at $r=\sin\alpha$, since $r^2\cot^2\alpha=1-r^2$. Thus
$$
\begin{aligned}
V&=2\pi\int_0^{\sin\alpha}r\left(\sqrt{1-r^2}-r\cot\alpha\right)\,dr\\
&=\frac{2\pi}{3}\left[1-\cos^3\alpha-\sin^3\alpha\cot\alpha\right]
=\frac{2\pi}{3}(1-\cos\alpha).
\end{aligned}
$$
The equality uses $\sin^3\alpha\cot\alpha=\sin^2\alpha\cos\alpha$. The positive <cosine> on the stated range removes any square-root sign ambiguity.

