= Solution
{parent=i}

For a first-order <linear ordinary differential equation> $y'+p(x)y=q(x)$, an <integrating factor> is $\exp(\int p(x)\,dx)$. Here $p(x)=\cot x$, so
$$
\mu(x)=\exp(\ln\sin x)=\boxed{\sin x.}
$$
The <sine> is positive on the specified interval, so this <integrating factor> never vanishes there. Any nonzero constant multiple is equally suitable.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiplication by the <integrating factor> and the <product rule> turn the left side into a single <derivative>:
$$
(y\sin x)'=\sin x\cos^2x.
$$
Using <integration by substitution> $u=\cos x$ gives $y\sin x=-\cos^3x/3+C$. Therefore the <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{C-\frac13\cos^3x}{\sin x},\qquad0<x<\frac\pi2.}
$$
There is one arbitrary <constant of integration>, as expected for a first-order <ordinary differential equation>.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Insert the <exponential function> trial $y=e^{rx}$ into the <homogeneous linear differential equation>. Its <characteristic equation> is $r^2+\sqrt3r+3=0$, with <polynomial roots>
$$
r=-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\pm\frac32i.
$$
Taking real combinations of the two complex exponential solutions produces the <general solution>
$$
\boxed{y_c(x)=e^{-\sqrt3x/2}\left(A\cos\frac{3x}{2}+B\sin\frac{3x}{2}\right).}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <method of undetermined coefficients> with a <particular solution> $Ke^{-\sqrt3x}$. The differential operator multiplies this <exponential function> by $3-3+3=3$. Matching the forcing coefficient gives $3K=1$, hence
$$
\boxed{y_{p,1}(x)=\frac13e^{-\sqrt3x}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A linear <polynomial> trial $y=ux+v$ has $y'=u$ and $y''=0$. Substituting gives $3ux+(\sqrt3u+3v)=x$, so $u=1/3$ and $v=-\sqrt3/9=-1/(3\sqrt3)$. Thus a <particular solution> is
$$
\boxed{y_{p,2}(x)=\frac x3-\frac1{3\sqrt3}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <superposition principle> gives the <general solution> as $y_c+2y_{p,2}+y_{p,1}$:
$$
y=e^{-\sqrt3x/2}\left(A\cos\frac{3x}{2}+B\sin\frac{3x}{2}\right)+\frac{2x}{3}-\frac2{3\sqrt3}+\frac13e^{-\sqrt3x}.
$$
The first <boundary condition> gives $A=2/(3\sqrt3)-1/3$. At $x=\pi$, the <cosine> vanishes and the <sine> equals $-1$. Comparing with the second <boundary condition> then gives
$$
-Be^{-\sqrt3\pi/2}+\frac{2\pi}{3}=0,
\qquad B=\frac{2\pi}{3}e^{\sqrt3\pi/2}.
$$
The unique solution satisfying both <boundary conditions> is therefore
$$
\boxed{y=e^{-\sqrt3x/2}\left[\left(\frac2{3\sqrt3}-\frac13\right)\cos\frac{3x}{2}+\frac{2\pi}{3}e^{\sqrt3\pi/2}\sin\frac{3x}{2}\right]+\frac{2x}{3}-\frac2{3\sqrt3}+\frac13e^{-\sqrt3x}.}
$$
Uniqueness follows because a <homogeneous solution> zero at both endpoints would have $A=0$ from the first and $B=0$ from the second.

= 14
{parent=Paper 1}
