= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $s=x^2+y^2$. The <gradient> components are
$$
f_x=4x(s-1),\qquad f_y=y\left[4(s-1)+\frac7{3+y^2}\right].
$$
A <stationary point> requires both components to vanish. If $x\ne0$, then $s=1$; the second component subsequently forces $y=0$, giving $(\pm1,0)$. If $x=0$, either $y=0$ or, writing $u=y^2$,
$$
4(u-1)(3+u)+7=4u^2+8u-5=(2u-1)(2u+5)=0.
$$
Only $u=1/2$ is nonnegative. Thus there are five <stationary points>, with values
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
(\pm1,0):&\quad f=\frac72\ln3,\\
(0,0):&\quad f=1+\frac72\ln3,\\
(0,\pm1/\sqrt2):&\quad f=\frac14+\frac72\ln\frac72.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The cases $x=0$ and $s=1$ exhaust the first <partial derivative> equation, so no other <stationary points> have been lost by division.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Hessian matrix> has entries
$$
f_{xx}=4(s-1)+8x^2,\qquad f_{xy}=8xy,\qquad f_{yy}=4(s-1)+8y^2+\frac{7(3-y^2)}{(3+y^2)^2}.
$$
At each <stationary point> the mixed entry vanishes, so the signs of the diagonal entries directly classify the local quadratic change:
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{point}&H&\text{nature}\\\hline
(\pm1,0)&\operatorname{diag}(8,7/3)&\text{strict local minima}\\
(0,0)&\operatorname{diag}(-4,-5/3)&\text{strict local maximum}\\
(0,\pm1/\sqrt2)&\operatorname{diag}(-2,24/7)&\text{saddle points}
\end{array}
$$
In particular the two <local minima> are also <global minima>: the squared term is nonnegative and $\ln(3+y^2)\ge\ln3$, with equality in both bounds exactly at $(\pm1,0)$. The <local maximum> at the origin is not a <global maximum>, since $f$ grows without bound as $x^2+y^2\to\infty$. The opposite diagonal signs at the other two points establish their <saddle point> character.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <contour plot> is symmetric under either $x\mapsto-x$ or $y\mapsto-y$, because $f$ depends only on their squares. Denote the three critical heights by $m=\tfrac72\ln3$, $h=\tfrac14+\tfrac72\ln(7/2)$ and $M=1+\tfrac72\ln3$, where $m<h<M$.

For $m<c<h$, the <level set> $f=c$ consists of separate closed curves around the two <minima>. At $c=h$ the branches meet at the two <saddle points>. For $h<c<M$, there is an inner closed curve surrounding the <local maximum> and an outer closed curve surrounding all five <stationary points>. At $c=M$ the inner curve shrinks to the origin; for $c>M$ only the outer closed curve remains. At each <saddle point> the critical <level set> crosses in the two directions determined by
$$
-\delta x^2+\frac{12}{7}\delta y^2=0,
\qquad\delta y=\pm\sqrt{\frac7{12}}\,\delta x.
$$
The figure plots representative <level sets>, including the critical saddle height; its second panel adds the <gradient> directions requested below.

\Image[paper-1-2020-q14-contours-gradient.png]
{title=Contour topology and gradient directions around two minima, a maximum and two saddles}

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <gradient> is <orthogonal> to each regular <level set> and points towards increasing $f$. Near either <minimum>, the linear approximation is $(8\delta x,\tfrac73\delta y)$, so arrows point away from the point. Near the <maximum> at the origin it is $(-4\delta x,-\tfrac53\delta y)$, so arrows point inwards. Near either <saddle point> it is $(-2\delta x,\tfrac{24}{7}\delta y)$: arrows point towards the vertical axis horizontally and away from the saddle vertically.

On the horizontal axis the <gradient> is $(4x(x^2-1),0)$. Its arrows point left on $x<-1$, right on $-1<x<0$, left on $0<x<1$, and right on $x>1$. On the vertical axis it is
$$
\nabla f(0,y)=\left(0,\frac{y(2y^2-1)(2y^2+5)}{3+y^2}\right).
$$
Consequently its arrows point down on $y<-1/\sqrt2$, up on $-1/\sqrt2<y<0$, down on $0<y<1/\sqrt2$, and up on $y>1/\sqrt2$. These directions, and representative arrows near each <stationary point>, appear in the right-hand panel. The <gradient> itself is zero at all five <stationary points>.

