= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Random <sampling without replacement> assigns each of the ten distinguishable pieces to each lunch with the same <probability>. Five pieces of each type therefore give
$$
\boxed{P(A_1)=\frac12,\qquad P(B_2)=\frac12.}
$$
One can check the second <probability> by conditioning on the first lunch:
$$
P(B_2)=P(B_2\mid A_1)P(A_1)+P(B_2\mid B_1)P(B_1)
=\frac59\frac12+\frac49\frac12=\frac12.
$$
The identical marginal <probabilities> do not imply <independence> of the draws.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Condition on an apple occupying position two in a uniformly random ordering. The nine other positions then contain four apples and five bananas, each equally likely to occupy position three. The <conditional probability> is therefore
$$
\boxed{P(A_3\mid A_2)=\frac49.}
$$
This uses <exchangeability> of the unconditioned positions; there is no need to assume the first lunch contained a particular fruit.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Each position has apple <probability> $1/2$. Applying <Bayes' theorem> to the preceding <conditional probability> gives
$$
\boxed{P(A_2\mid A_3)=\frac{P(A_3\mid A_2)P(A_2)}{P(A_3)}=\frac{(4/9)(1/2)}{1/2}=\frac49.}
$$
This also agrees with <exchangeability> of the second and third positions.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

By the <inclusion-exclusion principle for probabilities>,
$$
P(B_1\cup A_2)=P(B_1)+P(A_2)-P(B_1\cap A_2).
$$
After an initial banana, five of the nine remaining pieces are apples, so the intersection <probability> is $(1/2)(5/9)=5/18$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{P(B_1\cup A_2)=\frac12+\frac12-\frac5{18}=\frac{13}{18}.}
$$
As a check, the complementary event is an apple followed by a banana, which also has <probability> $5/18$.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Let $I_j$ be the <indicator random variable> of an apple in lunch $j$. The number remaining is $R=5-I_1-I_2-I_3$. Each <expected value> $E[I_j]=P(A_j)=1/2$. The <linearity of expectation> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{E[R]=5-3\left(\frac12\right)=\frac72\text{ apples}.}
$$
Although the <indicator random variables> are dependent under <sampling without replacement>, the <linearity of expectation> needs no <independence> assumption.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $N$ count rotten pieces. Each item supplies an independent <Bernoulli trial> with success <probability> $p$, so $N$ has a <binomial distribution> with parameters $10,p$. There are $\binom{10}{n}$ choices of the rotten items, and each such choice has <probability> $p^n(1-p)^{10-n}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{P(N=n)=\binom{10}{n}p^n(1-p)^{10-n},\qquad n=0,\ldots,10.}
$$
For $p=0$ or $p=1$, interpret this as the corresponding distribution concentrated at zero or ten; no ambiguous $0^0$ evaluation is needed.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $N=\sum_{j=1}^{10}X_j$, where the independent <Bernoulli random variables> indicate rottenness. Since $X_j^2=X_j$, their <variances> are $E[X_j^2]-E[X_j]^2=p-p^2$. The <independence> makes all cross <covariances> zero, so
$$
\operatorname{Var}(N)=10p(1-p),\qquad\boxed{\sigma_N=\sqrt{10p(1-p)}\text{ pieces}.}
$$
This <standard deviation> vanishes at $p=0$ and $p=1$, when the count is certain.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
