= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <integration by substitution> $u=\cos x$, $du=-\sin x\,dx$. The denominator factorizes as $(u-2)(u-3)$, and <partial fraction decomposition> gives
$$
-\int\frac{du}{(u-2)(u-3)}=\int\left(\frac1{u-2}-\frac1{u-3}\right)\,du.
$$
Integrating and restoring $x$ yields
$$
\boxed{\int\frac{\sin x}{\cos^2x-5\cos x+6}\,dx=\ln\frac{2-\cos x}{3-\cos x}+C.}
$$
For real $x$, both $2-\cos x$ and $3-\cos x$ are positive, so no absolute-value signs are needed in this real <logarithm>. The denominator never vanishes.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For <integration by parts>, choose $u=\ln x$ and $dv=x^{-4}\,dx$. Then $du=dx/x$ and $v=-1/(3x^3)$, giving
$$
\int\frac{\ln x}{x^4}\,dx=-\frac{\ln x}{3x^3}+\frac13\int x^{-4}\,dx
=\boxed{-\frac{3\ln x+1}{9x^3}+C,\qquad x>0.}
$$
Here $C$ is an arbitrary <constant of integration>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Choose the <trigonometric substitution> $x=\sin u$ with $-\pi/2\le u\le\pi/2$. The <cosine> is nonnegative there, so $\sqrt{1-x^2}=\cos u$ and $dx=\cos u\,du$. The <double-angle formula> then gives
$$
\int\sqrt{1-x^2}\,dx=\int\cos^2u\,du=\frac u2+\frac14\sin2u+C.
$$
Since $u=\arcsin x$ and $\sin2u=2x\sqrt{1-x^2}$,
$$
\boxed{\int\sqrt{1-x^2}\,dx=\frac12\left(\arcsin x+x\sqrt{1-x^2}\right)+C.}
$$
The formula is an <antiderivative> on $(-1,1)$ and has continuous endpoint values at $\pm1$.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The product in the integrand simplifies to $\sin x\cos^3x$ below its upper endpoint. With the <integration by substitution> $t=\cos x$, the limits become $c$ and zero and $dt=-\sin x\,dx$. Therefore
$$
I=-\int_c^0t^3\,dt=\int_0^ct^3\,dt=J,
\qquad\boxed{I=J=\frac{c^4}{4}.}
$$
Although the <tangent function> itself is undefined at the upper endpoint, the product has a zero <limit> there, so its continuous extension, or its <improper integral>, gives this same result.

To obtain $J$ directly from a <Riemann sum>, divide $[0,c]$ into $n$ equal pieces of width $c/n$ and use right endpoints $t_k=kc/n$. The sum is
$$
\sum_{k=1}^n\left(\frac{kc}{n}\right)^3\frac cn
=\frac{c^4}{n^4}\frac{n^2(n+1)^2}{4}
=\frac{c^4}{4}\left(1+\frac1n\right)^2.
$$
The integrand is continuous, so the <limit> of these <Riemann sums> is its <definite integral>, namely $c^4/4$, agreeing with the <antiderivative> calculation.

