= Solution
{parent=i}

Put $d=c-1$. The <matrix> is real and symmetric, so <orthogonality> means $A^{\mathsf T}A=A^2=I$. Multiplication gives the scalar conditions
$$
a^2+d^2=1,\qquad b^2=1,\qquad2ad=0.
$$
If $d=0$, then $c=1$ and $a=\pm1$; if $a=0$, then $d=\pm1$ and $c=0$ or $2$. In both cases $b=\pm1$. The eight parameter triples are therefore
$$
\boxed{\begin{gathered}
(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}\in\{(1,1,1)^{\mathsf T},(1,-1,1)^{\mathsf T},(-1,1,1)^{\mathsf T},(-1,-1,1)^{\mathsf T},\\
(0,1,0)^{\mathsf T},(0,-1,0)^{\mathsf T},(0,1,2)^{\mathsf T},(0,-1,2)^{\mathsf T}\}.
\end{gathered}}
$$
These conditions are sufficient as well as necessary, since they make every entry of $A^{\mathsf T}A$ equal to the corresponding entry of the <identity matrix>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A three-dimensional <rotation matrix> is an <orthogonal matrix> with <determinant> $+1$. Here $\det A=b(a^2-d^2)$. For the branch $d=0$, the determinant condition gives $b=1$; for the branch $a=0$, it gives $b=-1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}\in\{(1,1,1)^{\mathsf T},(-1,1,1)^{\mathsf T},(0,-1,0)^{\mathsf T},(0,-1,2)^{\mathsf T}\}.}
$$
The first is the identity <rotation>; the remaining three are half-turns. Their fixed axes are respectively the $y$ axis, the direction $(1,0,-1)$ and the direction $(1,0,1)$. The identity is included under the usual <rotation matrix> convention.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A square homogeneous <linear system> has a nonzero solution exactly when its <determinant> vanishes. Expanding along the second row gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\det A&=3\left[(18-\lambda)+2\lambda(1-\lambda)\right]
-3\left[(9-\lambda)+\lambda(2-\lambda)\right]\\
&=3(9-\lambda^2).
\end{aligned}
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\lambda=3\quad\text{or}\quad\lambda=-3.}
$$
For an explicit check, the <null space> contains $(3,-1,1)^{\mathsf T}$ at $\lambda=3$ and $(3,1,-1)^{\mathsf T}$ at $\lambda=-3$.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The original PDF requires $B$ itself to be an <orthogonal skew-symmetric matrix>; it does not require $C$ to be diagonal. A real two-dimensional <skew-symmetric matrix> has the form $B=\begin{pmatrix}0&u\\-u&0\end{pmatrix}$. The <orthogonal matrix> condition gives $u^2=1$, so choose $u=1$. Then $C=B^{\mathsf T}AB$ is forced:
$$
\boxed{B=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad C=\begin{pmatrix}-4&-3\\-3&4\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Direct multiplication verifies $B^{\mathsf T}=-B$, $B^{\mathsf T}B=I$ and $BCB^{\mathsf T}=A$. Choosing $-B$ gives the same $C$. Replacing $B$ by a matrix of <eigenvectors> to make $C$ diagonal would generally fail the printed antisymmetry requirement.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the $C$ obtained above, the <characteristic polynomial> is
$$
\det(C-\mu I)=(-4-\mu)(4-\mu)-9=\mu^2-25.
$$
Thus the <eigenvalues> are $5$ and $-5$. For $\mu=5$, $(C-5I)\mathbf v=0$ gives $v_2=-3v_1$; for $\mu=-5$, it gives $v_1=3v_2$. Normalizing the <eigenvectors> yields
$$
\boxed{\mu=5:\ \mathbf v_+=\frac1{\sqrt{10}}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-3\end{pmatrix};\qquad
\mu=-5:\ \mathbf v_-=\frac1{\sqrt{10}}\begin{pmatrix}3\\1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Both are <unit vectors>, their <dot product> is zero, and either overall sign is allowed for each <eigenvector>.

= 19
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In the <Cartesian coordinate system>, the <Laplacian> of a <scalar field> is the sum of its three unmixed second <partial derivatives>. Each squared coordinate here contributes one, so
$$
\boxed{\nabla^2\psi=\psi_{xx}+\psi_{yy}+\psi_{zz}=1+1+1=3.}
$$
Equivalently $\nabla\psi=(x,y,z)$ and its <divergence> is three.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The sloping height relation on the <cone> is $z=b(1-r/a)$. A <parametric equation> of its curved surface is therefore
$$
\mathbf R(r,\theta)=(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta,b(1-r/a)),\qquad0\le r\le a,\quad0\le\theta<2\pi.
$$
Its two <tangent vectors> are $\mathbf R_r=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta,-b/a)$ and $\mathbf R_\theta=r(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,0)$. Multiplication by $dr$ and $d\theta$ produces the two infinitesimal sides requested in the paper. Their <cross product> is the <vector area element>:
$$
\boxed{d\mathbf S=(\mathbf R_r\times\mathbf R_\theta)\,dr\,d\theta
=r\left(\frac ba\cos\theta,\frac ba\sin\theta,1\right)\,dr\,d\theta.}
$$
Its radial component is positive, so this <normal vector> points outwards from the solid <cone>. Taking its <norm> gives the scalar <area element>
$$
\boxed{dS=r\sqrt{1+\frac{b^2}{a^2}}\,dr\,d\theta.}
$$
The <parametric equation> degenerates at the apex, but that single point contributes no <area>.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Compute the <flux> directly using the outward <vector area element>, as required. Restrict the <vector field> to the surface <parametric equation> and take the <dot product>:
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf R)\cdot d\mathbf S
=\left[\frac ba r^2(\cos^2\theta+\sin^2\theta)+br\left(1-\frac ra\right)\right]\,dr\,d\theta
=br\,dr\,d\theta.
$$
The terms quadratic in $r$ cancel. Therefore the <surface integral> is
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^a br\,dr\,d\theta=\pi a^2b.}
$$
This is a direct evaluation of the <flux of the position vector through a cone>.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

On the <sphere> of radius $a$, the outward <unit normal> is $\mathbf n=(x,y,z)/a$. Thus $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n=a$ everywhere. Multiplying by the <surface area> $4\pi a^2$ gives
$$
\boxed{\Phi=\int\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=4\pi a^3.}
$$
The same <surface integral> follows in <spherical coordinates> from $dS=a^2\sin\vartheta\,d\vartheta\,d\varphi$.

= e
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The new <cone> can be parametrized by
$$
\mathbf R'(r,\theta)=(r\cos\theta,b(1-r/a),r\sin\theta).
$$
The outward <vector area element> is $r(\tfrac ba\cos\theta,1,\tfrac ba\sin\theta)\,dr\,d\theta$. This is $\mathbf R'_\theta\times\mathbf R'_r\,dr\,d\theta$; reversing the order is needed to retain the outward orientation after the exchange of coordinates. Its <dot product> with $\mathbf F(\mathbf R')$ is again $br\,dr\,d\theta$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_{C'}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf S=\pi a^2b.}
$$
This equality also follows because a rigid <rotation> takes the first <cone> into the second and leaves the radial <vector field> $\mathbf F(\mathbf x)=\mathbf x$ invariant.

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Parametrize the segment in its stated direction by $\mathbf x(t)=(1-t,t,t)$, $0\le t\le1$. Then $d\mathbf x=(-1,1,1)\,dt$, and substitution into the two <vector fields> gives
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf x(t))=(2t,1,1),\qquad
\mathbf G(\mathbf x(t))=(2t^2,1-2t+2t^2,1-2t+2t^2).
$$
Taking each <dot product> with $d\mathbf x$ evaluates the <line integrals>:
$$
\boxed{\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf x=\int_0^1(2-2t)\,dt=1,\qquad
\int\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf x=\int_0^1(2-4t+2t^2)\,dt=\frac23.}
$$

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The proposed <parametric equation> traces precisely the same oriented segment, with a different speed. Set $u=t^n$, which increases from zero to one, and $du=nt^{n-1}\,dt$. Direct substitution gives the <line integrals>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf x&=\int_0^1(2-2t^n)nt^{n-1}\,dt=\int_0^1(2-2u)\,du=1,\\
\int\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf x&=\int_0^1(2-4t^n+2t^{2n})nt^{n-1}\,dt=\int_0^1(2-4u+2u^2)\,du=\frac23.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus **both values are unchanged**. The <derivative> of $u$ vanishes at the initial endpoint for $n>1$, but the monotone <integration by substitution> is still valid. This is invariance under <reparametrization> of one curve, which applies even to a nonconservative <vector field>; it is not evidence of general path independence.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The three components of the <curl> cancel pairwise:
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf F=(1-1,1-1,1-1)=\mathbf0.
$$
The domain is all of $\mathbb R^3$, which is <simply connected>, so this is consistent with a <conservative vector field>. To construct its <scalar potential>, integrate $\Phi_x=y+z$ to obtain $\Phi=x(y+z)+h(y,z)$. Matching $\Phi_y=x+z$ gives $h_y=z$, hence $h=yz+k(z)$. Finally $\Phi_z=x+y$ forces $k'(z)=0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\Phi(x,y,z)=xy+xz+yz+C,\qquad\mathbf F=\nabla\Phi.}
$$
Every <scalar potential> differs by a constant on this connected domain. The endpoint values differ by one, agreeing with the <line integral> in part (a).

= d
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the second <vector field>, the <curl> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf G=\bigl(2y-2z,\ 2z-2x,\ 2x-2y\bigr).}
$$
It is nonzero, for example $(0,-2,2)$ at $(1,0,0)$. A differentiable <scalar potential> would have zero <curl> of its <gradient>, so **$\mathbf G$ is not conservative**. The fact that its <curl> vanishes on the line $x=y=z$ does not make it vanish throughout the domain.

= e
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Take a piecewise linear <path> along three coordinate directions, passing through the origin and then $(0,1,0)$:
$$
\mathbf x(t)=\begin{cases}(1-t,0,0),&0\le t\le1,\\(0,t-1,0),&1\le t\le2,\\(0,1,t-2),&2\le t\le3.\end{cases}
$$
On the first segment $G_x=y^2+z^2=0$, so its <line integral> is zero. On the second $G_y=x^2+z^2=0$, so its contribution is also zero. On the last segment $G_z=x^2+y^2=1$ and $z$ increases from zero to one. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf x=0+0+\int_0^1 1\,dz=1\ne\frac23.}
$$
This gives the requested concrete path dependence with the same ordered endpoints.
